#IDOR Vulnerability Report: crAPI Mechanic Reports

Title: Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) in Mechanic Reports allows accessing any user's repair reports. 
Target: http://localhost:8888 (crAPI) 
Severity: High 
Vulnerability Class: Broken Object Level Authorization

### Summary 
Attackers can access any mechanic repair report by manipulating the report_id parameter, regardless of ownership. Sensitive data including owner emails, phone numbers, VINs, and problem details are exposed to unauthorized users.
  
### Steps to Reproduce

1. Authenticate with any user token 
2. After adding a vehicle, we will have an option to send service request to mechanic by using the Contact Mechanic option.
3. Observe the request sent after the Send Service Request. In the response of /workshop/api/merchant/contact_mechanic, 
4. we will be able to find this hidden API endpoint ( /workshop/api/mechanic/mechanic_report?report_id={id}).
5. Change the value of report_id in the request and send it to access mechanic reports of other users.
6. Receive another user's: emails, phones, VINs, problem details

### Impact
1. GDPR violation,
2. PII exposure 
3. user reports accessible, sensitive data fully exposed

### Root Cause
No ownership verification in endpoint handler - user-supplied ID used directly in database query without checking against authenticated user's ID.

### Remediation
Add ownership check: `filter_by(id=report_id, owner_id=current_user.id)` before returning data. Return 404/403 when access denied. Add audit logging, input validation, and security headers.
